@@ -0,0 +1,532 @@
+# Independent adversarial audit: 81-numerical-letter-grade
+
+## Executive decision
+
+The candidate contains several genuine, freshly reproducible K reachability
+proofs, but it does not contain a proof of the requested function for an
+arbitrary input list. Its only top-level symbolic theorems cover the empty list
+and a list of length one; the only longer top-level theorem is the single
+concrete prompt example. The two loop-step claims establish one iteration at a
+time but no claim states or proves the arbitrary-list result. The unused
+`expectedGrades` function makes this omission especially visible.
+
+There is also a real generated-semantics mismatch. `semantic.k` assigns exact
+decimal rational meanings to Python float literals. When a runtime Python float
+is represented by its actual exact binary ratio, the K execution disagrees with
+both Python implementations at intended threshold inputs 3.7, 2.7, and 1.3.
+Thus the successful claims concern a narrower exact-decimal abstraction, with
+no proved connection to the real Python float execution.
+
+These are material adequacy and real-program-model failures. Successful fresh
+`#Top` results and a successful non-vacuity probe do not repair them.
+
+## 1. Input and provenance integrity
+
+### Mode boundary
+
+The rendered mode is `GENERATED_SEMANTICS`. `/reference` contains exactly the
+three declared trusted regular files:
+
+- `/reference/canonical.py`
+- `/reference/prompt.py`
+- `/reference/py2mpy.py`
+
+`/reference/reference-semantics` is absent, as required. There is no
+infrastructure-mode contradiction, so a candidate verdict is appropriate.
+See [01-integrity.log](evidence/01-integrity.log).
+
+### Candidate artifact integrity
+
+All candidate source and provenance artifacts required for this audit are
+present as regular files: `run-input.json`, `metrics.json`, `codex-last.txt`,
+`codex-output.log`, `prompt.py`, `py2mpy.py`, `semantic.k`, `solution.py`,
+`solution.mpy`, `spec.k`, `verification.k`, and `prove.sh`. The structured
+trace is present at
+`/candidate/codex-trace/2026/07/22/rollout-2026-07-22T05-49-57-019f8972-4f98-7280-a36c-d3732b1bc74c.jsonl`.
+None of these required artifacts is missing, mistyped, or symlinked.
+
+The candidate prompt and translator compare byte-identically with their
+trusted mounted versions:
+
+- prompt SHA-256:
+  `489ff1f658e105a21a1e28c105082983a77af3a6b0576d9cdfb43745de1b507c`
+- translator SHA-256:
+  `406485eac118431c08ef59f30f79dfa6aa9d5825408917fc5ce7c806e664db16`
+
+The candidate also contains extra generated directories
+`semantic-kompiled/`, `verification-kompiled/`, and `__pycache__/`. They are
+not source-integrity failures, but they were treated as untrusted and never
+used. Only the source files copied to `/tmp/audit-work/candidate-src` were
+compiled. The scratch copies hash-identically to their candidate sources; the
+hash ledger is in [01-integrity.log](evidence/01-integrity.log).
+
+### Untrusted generation claims
+
+I read the complete five provenance inputs. The bounded full-file summary,
+hashes, parsed JSONL record counts, and relevant claimed commands are in
+[20-provenance-summary.log](evidence/20-provenance-summary.log), generated by
+[provenance_summary.py](evidence/provenance_summary.py).
+
+`run-input.json` claims problem 81, condition `bare`, and no supplied semantics.
+`metrics.json` claims exit 0 without timeout. `codex-last.txt`,
+`codex-output.log`, and the 237-record trace claim that all proofs closed.
+Those statements were used only as provenance claims. No candidate log,
+compiled definition, trace, or prior `#Top` was accepted as proof evidence.
+
+Stage 1 result: PASS.
+
+## 2. Program fidelity and candidate-versus-canonical checks
+
+### Natural-language contract
+
+For each GPA in the input list, return a letter in the same position using this
+strict decision table:
+
+| Condition, in branch order | Grade |
+|---|---|
+| `gpa == 4.0` | `A+` |
+| `gpa > 3.7` | `A` |
+| `gpa > 3.3` | `A-` |
+| `gpa > 3.0` | `B+` |
+| `gpa > 2.7` | `B` |
+| `gpa > 2.3` | `B-` |
+| `gpa > 2.0` | `C+` |
+| `gpa > 1.7` | `C` |
+| `gpa > 1.3` | `C-` |
+| `gpa > 1.0` | `D+` |
+| `gpa > 0.7` | `D` |
+| `gpa > 0.0` | `D-` |
+| otherwise | `E` |
+
+The strict inequalities mean, for example, that exactly 3.7 is `A-`, exactly
+3.0 is `B`, exactly 0.7 is `D-`, and exactly 0.0 is `E`. The prompt does not
+state a formal 0-to-4 precondition; the trusted canonical function applies the
+same branch chain to any ordered numeric input.
+
+### Source and translation
+
+`/candidate/solution.py` is the same algorithm as the trusted canonical
+function, with only names and prose changed. It preserves input order and emits
+one result per element.
+
+I regenerated the constructor program with the trusted copied translator:
+
+```text
+python3 /tmp/audit-work/reference/py2mpy.py /tmp/audit-work/candidate-src/solution.py > /tmp/audit-work/build/regenerated-solution.mpy
+cmp -- /tmp/audit-work/build/regenerated-solution.mpy /tmp/audit-work/candidate-src/solution.mpy
+```
+
+Both commands exited 0, and both files have SHA-256
+`a2c515dd246ef12031f0d3a266f7331002216c533423df82fdb0cbb97b75f246`.
+See [02-translation-fidelity.log](evidence/02-translation-fidelity.log).
+
+### Independent differential test
+
+[differential_test.py](evidence/differential_test.py) independently imports
+the trusted canonical entry point and the candidate entry point. It records
+every input in [differential-inputs.json](evidence/differential-inputs.json).
+The scope was:
+
+- the documented five-element example;
+- empty input;
+- every exact branch boundary;
+- integer boundary forms;
+- values below 0 and above 4;
+- the machine float immediately below and above each of the 12 thresholds;
+- 200 deterministic generated lists, lengths 0 through 16, with values sampled
+  uniformly over `[0.0, 4.0]` using seed 810081.
+
+The run checked 217 lists and 1,711 GPA values with zero mismatches. Exact
+command, result, and exit 0 are in [03-differential.log](evidence/03-differential.log).
+This establishes strong finite source-fidelity evidence; it is not a K theorem.
+
+Stage 2 result: PASS.
+
+## 3. Clean proof reconstruction
+
+### Fresh toolchain and builds
+
+The live toolchain is K v7.1.293; see [00-toolchain.log](evidence/00-toolchain.log).
+No candidate-built definition or cache was copied or referenced.
+
+The generated semantics was freshly compiled from `semantic.k` alone:
+
+```text
+kompile semantic.k --backend haskell --main-module MPY \
+  --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \
+  --output-definition /tmp/audit-work/build/semantic-kompiled
+```
+
+Exit: 0. Evidence: [04-kompile-semantic.log](evidence/04-kompile-semantic.log).
+
+The proof definition was independently compiled from `verification.k`, which
+requires the copied `semantic.k`:
+
+```text
+kompile verification.k --backend haskell --main-module VERIFICATION \
+  --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \
+  --output-definition /tmp/audit-work/build/verification-kompiled
+```
+
+Exit: 0. Evidence:
+[09-kompile-verification.log](evidence/09-kompile-verification.log).
+
+### Concrete generated-semantics execution
+
+Using the semantics-only definition, `krun` reached `.K` on:
+
+- the prompt example, returning `["A+", "B", "C-", "C", "A-"]`;
+- empty input, returning `[]`;
+- a 25-value branch and threshold traversal, returning every expected branch
+  in order.
+
+The independent Python results are in
+[05-python-concrete-oracle.log](evidence/05-python-concrete-oracle.log).
+The matching K configurations and exit statuses are in
+[06-krun-normal.log](evidence/06-krun-normal.log),
+[07-krun-empty.log](evidence/07-krun-empty.log), and
+[08-krun-boundaries.log](evidence/08-krun-boundaries.log).
+These comparisons use the candidate's exact-decimal input encoding.
+
+### Positive claims, run separately
+
+Every claim was selected and run independently against the fresh proof
+definition. Each command exited 0 and printed `#Top`:
+
+| Claim | Evidence |
+|---|---|
+| `SPEC.empty-input` | [10-kprove-empty-input.log](evidence/10-kprove-empty-input.log) |
+| `SPEC.all-single-grades` | [10-kprove-all-single-grades.log](evidence/10-kprove-all-single-grades.log) |
+| `SPEC.loop-step-new-variable` | [10-kprove-loop-step-new-variable.log](evidence/10-kprove-loop-step-new-variable.log) |
+| `SPEC.loop-step-existing-variable` | [10-kprove-loop-step-existing-variable.log](evidence/10-kprove-loop-step-existing-variable.log) |
+| `SPEC.loop-empty` | [10-kprove-loop-empty.log](evidence/10-kprove-loop-empty.log) |
+| `SPEC.prompt-example` | [10-kprove-prompt-example.log](evidence/10-kprove-prompt-example.log) |
+
+The aggregate command also exited 0 with `#Top`; see
+[11-kprove-all.log](evidence/11-kprove-all.log).
+
+Stage 3 result: PASS for reconstruction and closure. Closure is assessed for
+scope and soundness below.
+
+## 4. Adequacy and real-program pinning
+
+### Plain-language claim inventory
+
+1. `empty-input` starts `solutionProgram` with an empty input, empty
+   environment, and no result. It requires termination with empty `grades` and
+   `result` bindings and an empty returned list.
+2. `all-single-grades` starts `solutionProgram` on exactly one `num(P,Q)`,
+   requires `Q > 0`, and requires the environment and returned list to contain
+   exactly `expectedGrade(P,Q)`.
+3. `loop-step-new-variable` starts directly at a loop head whose remaining
+   input begins with `num(P,Q)`, with no prior `grade` binding, arbitrary
+   original grades and arbitrary accumulated output. For `Q > 0`, it proves
+   one body execution appends `expectedGrade(P,Q)`, binds `grade`, and reaches
+   the loop on the arbitrary tail.
+4. `loop-step-existing-variable` proves the same one-iteration transition when
+   an old `grade` binding already exists.
+5. `loop-empty` proves that an empty loop term is consumed.
+6. `prompt-example` runs `solutionProgram` on the five concrete prompt inputs
+   and fixes the complete final environment and return list.
+
+The top-level result is constrained in all three entry claims; it is not a free
+variable, tautology, or implication-only postcondition. The loop-step results
+are also constrained via the exact update of the `result` binding.
+
+### Satisfiable preconditions and concrete substitutions
+
+All entry preconditions are realizable:
+
+- `empty-input`: input `.Vals`, environment `.Map`, result `noResult`;
+- `all-single-grades`: `P = 4`, `Q = 1`, producing `A+`;
+- `prompt-example`: the exact five rational inputs printed in the claim.
+
+The loop-step preconditions are realizable with `P = 4`, `Q = 1`,
+`REST = .Vals`, `ALL = .Vals`, and `OUT = .Vals`; the existing-variable
+variant may take any old grade value. `loop-empty` is ground-realizable
+immediately. The corresponding Python and K executions occur in the Stage 3
+normal, empty, and boundary logs.
+
+### Program pin
+
+The entry claims start from the proof-local nullary function
+`solutionProgram`, not directly from the parsed file. That helper does not
+summarize a result or skip execution: its sole equation expands to the module
+AST, and `gradingBody` expands to the complete submitted branch tree. An
+audit-only equality claim containing the literal submitted constructor tree
+closed with `#Top`; see [program-pin.k](evidence/program-pin.k) and
+[12c-program-pin.log](evidence/12c-program-pin.log). The earlier
+[12-program-pin.log](evidence/12-program-pin.log) and
+[12b-program-pin.log](evidence/12b-program-pin.log) preserve two reviewer
+drafting diagnostics (empty-list concrete syntax and unsupported bare
+functional claims); neither involved a candidate target claim.
+
+Static comparison also confirms that the docstring expression, empty-result
+assignment, loop, all 12 tests, 13 appends, and return match
+`solution.mpy`. Therefore the claims that do start from `solutionProgram` pin
+the submitted AST.
+
+### Material theorem-scope failure
+
+There is no entry claim of the form “for every finite input list, execution
+returns the elementwise expected-grade list.” `all-single-grades` is exactly a
+length-one theorem. The two loop claims stop after one iteration with a
+residual `loop(..., REST)`; they do not state a final result for arbitrary
+`REST`. `loop-empty` only handles the base term, and `prompt-example` is one
+ground five-element case.
+
+`verification.k` defines `expectedGrades(Vals)`, the natural summary needed by
+an arbitrary-list theorem, but `spec.k` never refers to it. This is confirmed
+by [21-symbol-usage.log](evidence/21-symbol-usage.log). An informal induction
+could be sketched from the step facts, but no K reachability claim states or
+machine-checks that induction. Consequently the returned value of the real
+function is not proved correct for lists of length 2, 3, 4, or arbitrary
+length, except for the one concrete five-element example.
+
+Stage 4 result: FAIL.
+
+## 5. Rule-by-rule static soundness review
+
+The line-addressed machine inventory and program-constructor counts are in
+[15-static-inventory.log](evidence/15-static-inventory.log), produced by
+[static_inventory.py](evidence/static_inventory.py).
+
+### Local syntax, configuration, and attributes
+
+`MPY-SYNTAX` declares:
+
+- `Module(Module(Stmts))`;
+- statement lists `Stmts`, string lists `Strings`, and `Params`;
+- statement constructors `FuncDef`, `Return`, `Assign`, `If`, `For`, `Expr`;
+- expression lists `Exprs`;
+- expression constructors `Name`, `Str`, `Float`, `ListExpr`, `Compare`,
+  `Attribute`, `Call`;
+- comparison-operation lists `CmpOps` and constructor `CmpOp`;
+- runtime values `num`, `str`, `bool`, `list`, `none`;
+- runtime lists `.Vals` and `Value :: Vals`;
+- results `noResult` or `Value`.
+
+`MPY` adds 12 control constructors: `exec`, `eval`, `store`, `discard`,
+`ifReady`, `forReady`, `loop`, `put`, `cmpRight`, `applyCmp`, `appendTo`, and
+`returnValue`. Its configuration has exactly the required `<k>`, `<input>`,
+`<env>`, and `<result>` cells. There is no heap, call stack, output, exception,
+or allocation cell.
+
+The five local `[function, total]` declarations are `append`,
+`expectedGrade`, `expectedGrades`, `gradingBody`, and `solutionProgram`. There
+are no local `[functional]`, `[simplification]`, `[concrete]`, `[opaque]`, or
+explicit priority declarations. No fresh or opaque result-bearing oracle is
+present.
+
+### `semantic.k`: all 41 rules
+
+| Lines | Rules and assessment |
+|---|---|
+| 74-75 | `append(.Vals,V)` and recursive `append(V::VS,W)`. Complete over `Vals`, structurally decreasing, order-preserving, and sound. |
+| 78-81 | Exact module entry for the one submitted function. It binds `grades` to the input and executes the actual body. The match is intentionally minimal and exact for this program. |
+| 84-85 | Empty and nonempty statement sequencing. These preserve source order. |
+| 87-88 | Expression statements evaluate then discard a runtime value. This correctly handles the docstring and append-return `none`. |
+| 90-91 | Assignment evaluates the RHS before storing to a named target. Correct for the one used assignment. |
+| 93-97 | Existing-key update and absent-key insertion. The `in_keys` guard makes the cases disjoint and preserves all other map bindings. |
+| 99-101 | Test evaluation followed by true/false branch selection. Correct evaluation order and branch control for the program. |
+| 103-107 | Iterable evaluation, conversion of a list value to `loop`, empty-loop exit, and nonempty-loop binding/body/tail sequencing. The loop variable persists after iterations as in Python. |
+| 109-111 | Return-expression evaluation and result-cell update. This does not implement abrupt return from an arbitrary trailing continuation. In the submitted program `Return` is the final top-level statement and never occurs in a loop or branch, so no false conclusion for this real program and intended inputs follows. It is an over-broad reusable-semantics limitation, not an execution bypass in these claims. |
+| 114, 116, 117 | Name lookup, string evaluation, and the only used empty-list literal. All are correct on reachable program states. Nonempty list literals are deliberately unmodeled and unused. |
+| 120-131 | Twelve float-literal rules: `4.0 -> 4/1`, `3.7 -> 37/10`, `3.3 -> 33/10`, `3.0 -> 3/1`, `2.7 -> 27/10`, `2.3 -> 23/10`, `2.0 -> 2/1`, `1.7 -> 17/10`, `1.3 -> 13/10`, `1.0 -> 1/1`, `0.7 -> 7/10`, and `0.0 -> 0/1`. These are exact-decimal abstractions, not Python binary-float meanings. The concrete false-behavior witnesses are detailed below. |
+| 133-136 | Evaluate comparison left operand before right operand and retain the left value in `applyCmp`. This matches Python evaluation order for the used pure expressions. |
+| 137-140 | Rational equality by cross multiplication and greater-than as `P*B > A*Q`, where `P/Q` is the saved left operand and `A/B` the right. These are mathematically correct when denominators are positive, which every symbolic grade claim requires. The semantics itself does not reject nonpositive input denominators, so behavior outside that formal domain is not justified. |
+| 144-147 | Evaluate the append argument, append to the named environment list, and return `none`. This preserves order and the used mutation. It omits general method dispatch and aliasing, neither of which the submitted program exercises. |
+
+All `solution.mpy` constructors are mapped: `Module`/`FuncDef` by module entry;
+statement list, `Expr`, `Assign`, `For`, `If`, and `Return` by their sequencing
+rules; `Name`, `Str`, `Float`, and empty `ListExpr` by expression rules;
+`Compare`/`CmpOp` by comparison rules; and `Call(Attribute(Name(...),
+"append"),...)` by the append rules. The exact constructor counts are in
+[15-static-inventory.log](evidence/15-static-inventory.log).
+
+### `verification.k`: all 17 rules
+
+`expectedGrade` has 13 guarded equations, in order:
+
+1. `P = 4Q` gives `A+`;
+2. `P != 4Q` and `10P > 37Q` gives `A`;
+3. `37Q >= 10P > 33Q` gives `A-`;
+4. `33Q >= 10P > 30Q` gives `B+`;
+5. `30Q >= 10P > 27Q` gives `B`;
+6. `27Q >= 10P > 23Q` gives `B-`;
+7. `23Q >= 10P > 20Q` gives `C+`;
+8. `20Q >= 10P > 17Q` gives `C`;
+9. `17Q >= 10P > 13Q` gives `C-`;
+10. `13Q >= 10P > 10Q` gives `D+`;
+11. `10Q >= 10P > 7Q` gives `D`;
+12. `7Q >= 10P` and `P > 0` gives `D-`;
+13. `P <= 0` gives `E`.
+
+For `Q > 0` these guards are complete, pairwise disjoint, and exactly state
+the mathematical table. `expectedGrade` is a definitional postcondition
+summary; it does not replace execution. The body is symbolically executed
+before the result is matched to this function, so this is not a smuggled
+oracle.
+
+Outside the claimed domain, guards overlap. At `P=0,Q=-1`, both the `A` guard
+and the `E` guard are true; backend function ordering selects `A`.
+[outside-domain-overlap.k](evidence/outside-domain-overlap.k) and
+[16-overlap-to-a.log](evidence/16-overlap-to-a.log) record that reduction;
+[17-overlap-to-e.log](evidence/17-overlap-to-e.log) records rejection of the
+opposite result. Because every candidate use that affects a claim requires
+`Q > 0`, this is a global reuse/definition gap, not an intended-domain
+unsoundness witness and not a verdict driver.
+
+`expectedGrades` has two equations: empty maps to empty, and a numeric head
+maps through `expectedGrade` recursively. The recursion descends. Its
+`[total]` attribute is nevertheless too broad because the declared `Vals`
+sort also admits `str`, `bool`, `list`, and `none` heads, for which there is no
+equation. The symbol is unused by every claim, as
+[21-symbol-usage.log](evidence/21-symbol-usage.log) shows. This is a static
+quality defect, not a source of the successful results.
+
+`gradingBody` has one unguarded nullary equation to the complete actual branch
+tree. `solutionProgram` has one unguarded nullary equation to the complete
+actual module AST. Both are complete, nonrecursive definitional macros. They
+name code and then let the ordinary semantics execute it; neither summarizes
+or fabricates the result.
+
+### Concrete intended-domain false-behavior witnesses for float rules
+
+The abstract `num(P,Q)` datatype is documented as the exact rational `P/Q`.
+A faithful exact-rational representation of Python's runtime float `3.7` is:
+
+```text
+P = 4165829655317709
+Q = 1125899906842624
+```
+
+This satisfies the entry precondition `Q > 0`. Python compares that value to
+the identical binary float literal `3.7`, so equality holds and `>` is false;
+both trusted canonical and candidate Python return `A-`. In K, the literal
+rule makes the threshold exactly `37/10`, and
+`10P = 37Q + 2`, so the same exact runtime value is considered greater and K
+returns `A`.
+
+The same witness pattern occurs at:
+
+- Python `2.7` =
+  `3039929748475085/1125899906842624`: K returns `B`, Python returns `B-`;
+- Python `1.3` =
+  `5854679515581645/4503599627370496`: K returns `C-`, Python returns `D+`.
+
+The Python inputs, ratios, and results are preserved in
+[13-python-float-boundaries.log](evidence/13-python-float-boundaries.log).
+The K run on those exact positive-denominator ratios is in
+[14-krun-faithful-binary-float-ratios.log](evidence/14-krun-faithful-binary-float-ratios.log).
+The mismatch is enabled specifically by the float-literal rules at
+`semantic.k:121`, `semantic.k:124`, and `semantic.k:128`, together with the
+rational greater-than rule. These are intended table boundaries and satisfy
+the symbolic entry domain, so this is a concrete real-program false-conclusion
+witness, not merely an untested edge outside the theorem.
+
+One may instead choose an abstraction that maps the human decimal token 3.7
+to `37/10`; the ordinary concrete logs show that this mapping matches the
+table. But the candidate provides no abstraction relation or connection
+theorem from arbitrary runtime Python numeric values to that representation.
+Finite Python-versus-Python differential tests cannot supply that theorem.
+
+Stage 5 result: FAIL for real-Python semantic fidelity. The operational model
+is otherwise coherent on its declared exact-decimal, positive-denominator
+subset.
+
+## 6. Fresh non-vacuity test
+
+There is no candidate `spec-vacuity.k`. I created
+[spec-vacuity.k](evidence/spec-vacuity.k) in the audit evidence only. It uses
+the satisfiable input `[4.0]`, keeps the final environment correctly fixed to
+`result = ["A+"]`, and mutates only the returned result obligation to
+`["E"]`.
+
+The mutation parsed and built successfully under:
+
+```text
+kprove /audit-output/evidence/spec-vacuity.k \
+  -I /tmp/audit-work/candidate-src \
+  --definition /tmp/audit-work/build/verification-kompiled \
+  --spec-module SPEC-VACUITY --dry-run
+```
+
+Exit: 0. Evidence:
+[18-vacuity-dry-run.log](evidence/18-vacuity-dry-run.log).
+
+The same command without `--dry-run` exited 1 with
+`WarnStuckClaimState`. The residual is the fully terminated real execution:
+the environment and result both contain `A+`, which cannot unify with the
+mutated `E` destination. Evidence:
+[19-vacuity-proof.log](evidence/19-vacuity-proof.log).
+
+This is meaningful non-vacuity evidence: the reachable result obligation
+discriminates true from false. It validates the narrower ground/one-element
+proof shape, but it does not add the missing arbitrary-list theorem or repair
+the numeric semantics.
+
+Stage 6 result: PASS.
+
+## 7. Proven versus assumed accounting
+
+### What the successful K proofs establish
+
+Conditional on the candidate's exact-decimal rational semantics and the K
+builtins:
+
+- the submitted program returns an empty list on empty input;
+- for every `P,Q` with `Q > 0`, it returns the candidate table value on a
+  one-element `num(P,Q)` input;
+- one loop iteration appends that table value and reaches the next loop head,
+  both before and after the loop variable exists;
+- the empty loop consumes no computation;
+- the concrete prompt example returns the five stated grades in order.
+
+The positive claims are result-constraining, non-vacuous, and execute the
+actual pinned AST. There is no operational bridge that replaces the grading
+body with `expectedGrade`.
+
+They do **not** establish:
+
+- elementwise correctness for an arbitrary finite input list;
+- a top-level result of `expectedGrades(INPUT)` for arbitrary `INPUT`;
+- equivalence between exact-decimal `num` execution and real Python binary
+  float execution;
+- behavior for nonnumeric list elements, nonpositive rational denominators,
+  nonempty list literals, aliases, exceptions, or early returns with trailing
+  continuation;
+- termination as a separate total-correctness theorem.
+
+### Trust ledger
+
+| Boundary | Dependents | Assessment |
+|---|---|---|
+| K v7.1.293 compiler, Haskell backend, and builtin Bool/Int/Map operations | All builds and proofs | Ordinary low-level trusted proof infrastructure; freshly invoked and reproducibly logged. |
+| Trusted `/reference/py2mpy.py` | Program AST identity | Acceptable trusted input. Byte regeneration pins `solution.py` to `solution.mpy`. |
+| Human comparison of prompt/canonical behavior | Natural-language intent bridge | Supported strongly by the independent differential test, but finite evidence only. |
+| `append` and environment mutation rules | Loop claims and entry executions | Defined and reviewed operationally; no opaque primitive. |
+| `expectedGrade` equations for `Q>0` | Single-grade and loop-step postconditions | Acceptable mathematical specification function on the guarded domain; execution is independently performed. |
+| `gradingBody` and `solutionProgram` macros | All positive claims | Acceptable definitional naming: exact AST equality is checked and no result is summarized. |
+| Mapping Python numeric inputs to `num(P,Q)` | All conclusions advertised as about the Python function | Illegitimate as a universal real-program bridge. No relation or theorem is given, and faithful binary-float ratios provide concrete counterexamples. |
+| Informal induction from loop-step claims | Any asserted arbitrary-list correctness | Insufficient. It is not stated or discharged by a K entry claim. |
+| Python differential testing | Source/canonical equivalence on recorded cases | Useful finite evidence only; it does not replace either missing K theorem. |
+| `expectedGrades [total]` | None | Incorrectly broad declaration but unused; remove `[total]` or restrict its sort before reuse. |
+
+### Validation gates
+
+- Gate A, real-program soundness: FAIL. Program identity and non-vacuity pass,
+  but the numeric semantic bridge has concrete satisfying-input
+  counterexamples.
+- Gate B, intent adequacy: FAIL. No arbitrary-list entry theorem proves the
+  function contract.
+- Gate C, auditability: the reconstructed commands and finite tests are
+  reproducible, but the crucial Python-number abstraction has neither a
+  universal connection theorem nor correct boundary evidence.
+
+The candidate therefore proves honest but materially narrower facts. It is not
+a legitimate partial-correctness proof of the real generated function over its
+intended list input domain.
+
+VERDICT: FAIL
+LEGITIMACY: NOT_LEGIT
